germany's political parties have begun  exploratory talks to form a new  government that looks set to be a  three-way coalition two of the smaller  parties the greens and the pro-business  fdp have already held a first round of  joint talks now greens leaders are  meeting for their own inner party talks  to discuss the strategy for further  negotiations  autumn colors in berlin red yellow and  green the shades of the parties sounding  out a coalition  red for the spd green for the greens and  yellow for the pro-business ftp in a  so-called traffic light coalition  surveys say the majority of germans  favor that kind of alliance  chanan byram from the green party's left  wing has serious reservations  climate protection won't happen on its  own  how can we do that with a party that  says the market will sort things out i  don't know maybe i'm not creative enough  to imagine the compromises  of fantasy  green party leaders are walking a fine  line the greens and the free democrats  are worlds apart politically  tough compromises lie ahead that could  be hard to sell to the base  hey this canvas  we can create something new  wake up germany  something new can emerge from this  that's cool  if we go into the talks with this spirit  we can release new energy  that's what we want  in berlin many greens have doubts about  joining forces with the liberals but  after 16 years in opposition they're  also eager to get back into the fray  what motivates us is the desire to  finally get things done our time as an  opposition party is over  the base knows that we are now entering  negotiations and we have to make  compromises but that doesn't mean we're  selling out  now the greens must decide which  promises they could sacrifice  chanan bairam is wary of paying too high  a price  it's been revealed i was elected to  advocate on social issues particularly  on rents  we also need to bridge the gap between  rich and poor  we need to pay special attention to  these issues  but when i compare the party platforms  i just don't see how it could work with  the fdp  a traffic light alliance in germany will  demand a lot from all involved but it  could also come tumbling down  just like the autumn leaves do  eventually  we're here with chem ud samia a member  of parliament for the greens and former  parley leader uh mr uttamia how  optimistic are you about talks with the  fdp being successful  well it's first the liberals but we also  have the social democrats and in a  third round the christian democrats i'm  quite optimistic because this time  everybody wants success everybody is  aware about the responsibility we're the  fourth largest economy  other  countries are looking at germany how  fast we will build the new government  i'm quite optimistic that we managed to  have a government before christmas  in terms of finding common ground it  might be more difficult with the free  democrats when then with the spd for  example what uh differences do you think  might be a problem in finding agreement  of course we have uh big differences but  on the other side we also have some  things that we have in common we want to  modernize the country germany is lacking  behind regarding digitalization  they have a different approach with the  climate protection but they also accept  you know that we have to do something  and germany has to move ahead so maybe  we can find some common ground there as  well and on foreign policy  uh this is also very important vis-a-vis  the social democrats both parties are  against north stream too both parties  have a more critical view vis-a-vis mr  putin so that could be also some area  where we could move together ahead  and there's been some criticism of the  fact that the green team going into the  negotiation is not diverse is not very  diverse as someone uh like you with a  migration background what do you respond  and how do you see this criticism  well i don't criticize my party board i  was  myself a party leader and  i promised myself uh when i one day hand  over to the next leaders that i'm not  going to be the guy who is criticizing  them  so if i have to tell them anything i do  that directly not vis-a-vis media but  trust me  the  cabinet members will be much more  diverse than this team trust me on that  many thanks  joining us from the greens party  conference in berlin is our political  correspondent julia saldelli julia are  the greens any closer to agreeing to a  way forward  here at this party conference today the  greens have approved the team that will  go into the negotiations in the next few  days and weeks that's already an  important step and they have also  defined the guiding principles of what  they want to achieve we've heard  party co-leaders anna lena berbak and  nikola habek say they do want to be a  part of the next government and they do  want to put forward an important  ambitious  plan to fight climate change now they  are aware that to end up in a government  they will have to find an agreement with  the  business friendly fdp this is something  they are very much aware of and they are  ready to go into these talks and are  positive about the outcome  what about the overall political picture  in germany now where do things stand  so at the moment the social democrats  the center-left social democrats who  have come in first in the election are  sort of leading uh the talks and uh  going ahead and talking to  both the greens and the fdp and it seems  like a majority of germans are in favor  of this kind of three-party solution for  the next government but uh the  conservatives who have suffered an  important loss in the election who have  come in second still haven't really  given up they are also talking to the  smaller king maker parties and they'll  be ready to uh come into play again if  the coalition talks with the social  democrats do end up failing  all right political correspondent julia  saldelli speaking to us from the greens  party conference in berlin thanks very  much
 

